\description{
Assign deterministic confidence decisions to candidate burned patches
generated by the OtsuFire detection workflow.

This function performs the deterministic scoring stage of the pipeline.
It receives candidate burned patches produced by
\code{\link[=detect_burned_patches]{detect_burned_patches()}} and
evaluates them through the canonical rule-based decision system used
by OtsuFire.

The scoring workflow combines:
\enumerate{
  \item internal seed support and burnable-context plausibility
    (\strong{Filter 1}),
  \item optional active-fire hotspot corroboration
    (\strong{Filter 2}),
  \item temporal-consistency diagnostics against previous-year burned
    areas,
  \item spectral-support evaluation relative to a high-confidence
    burned-like reference distribution (\strong{Filter 3}).
}

These components are then integrated into a final deterministic
confidence decision assigning each candidate patch to `keep`,
`review`, or `drop`.

The function records the complete decision path of every candidate
patch, including filter outcomes, support metrics, temporal
diagnostics, and final decisions, producing a fully auditable
burned-area decision layer.

Validation against an external burned reference is not performed by
this function. Users should subsequently evaluate outputs with
\code{\link[=validate_fire_maps]{validate_fire_maps()}} or other
downstream validation steps.
}
\details{
\strong{The two supported Filter-3 reference modes}

Publicly, the package supports two ways of running \code{Filter 3}:

\enumerate{
  \item \strong{Same year, local fallback (`keep_pool = NULL`).}
  The scoring engine builds the spectral reference internally from the
  same target year being scored. It uses the early internal keep core
  defined by \code{flag_internal == "keep"} after the seed-support and
  burnable-context screening. This is the native automatic baseline.

  \item \strong{Other years, explicit keep pool.}
  The spectral reference is built outside the scorer from trusted
  decision layers belonging to other years. This is the cleanest
  explicit historical-reference mode.
}

A same-year \emph{explicit} \code{keep_pool} is intentionally not a
supported public mode. It is methodologically circular and, when the
helper-generated metadata reveal that the explicit pool contains the
target year, the scorer rejects it and instructs the user to use
\code{keep_pool = NULL} instead.

\strong{Rule-based deterministic scoring workflow}

Candidate burned patches are evaluated through a sequential decision
system designed to distinguish high-confidence burned patches from
ambiguous or spurious detections while preserving full decision
traceability.

\strong{Filter 1}

Filter 1 is derived from the internal Stage-2 support screening and
burnable-context checks.

Candidate patches intersecting Stage-1 support are assigned to the
\code{keep} pathway. Candidate patches without seed support are then
evaluated against the burnable domain:
\itemize{
  \item burnable but unseeded patches are assigned to \code{review},
  \item non-burnable patches are assigned to \code{drop},
  \item candidates with excessive missing vegetation / CORINE support
    may also be dropped.
}

Filter 1 is recorded through \code{filter_1} and \code{reason_1}.

\strong{Filter 2}

Filter 2 evaluates external corroboration using active-fire hotspots
when hotspot data are available for the target year.

In the current public implementation:
\itemize{
  \item hotspot-supported patches are mapped to `filter_2 = "keep"`,
  \item hotspot-free patches are mapped to `filter_2 = "review"`,
  \item when hotspot data are unavailable, Filter 2 is recorded as not
    applied.
}

Filter 2 is recorded through \code{filter_2} and \code{reason_2}.

\strong{Temporal consistency assessment}

Temporal consistency is evaluated separately from Filters 1 to 3.
Overlap with previous-year burned areas is used to diagnose residual
old scars, repeated detections, or temporal conflicts.

These diagnostics are recorded through:
\itemize{
  \item `preyear_overlap_frac`
  \item `preyear_action`
  \item `preyear_reason`
}

In the current canonical public implementation, this temporal branch
is preserved as an auditable companion diagnostic. The final public
field \code{class_final} is derived canonically from Filters 1, 2, and
3.

\strong{Filter 3}

Filter 3 evaluates spectral consistency relative to a high-confidence
keep-support distribution.

The workflow computes patch-level metrics such as:
\itemize{
  \item \code{percentile_in_keep}
  \item \code{p_above_keep_ref}
  \item \code{p_above_keep_q25}
  \item \code{area_ha}
  \item \code{n_pix}
  \item \code{conf_area}
  \item \code{conf_pix}
}

Patches are promoted to the spectral \code{keep} pathway only when
they satisfy the configured percentile and exceedance thresholds and
also pass the minimum area and minimum pixel checks.

In the current public implementation:
\itemize{
  \item spectrally well-supported patches become `filter_3 = "keep"`,
  \item borderline or weakly supported patches typically become
    `filter_3 = "review"`,
  \item `filter_3 = "drop"` is mainly reserved for failures of minimum
    geometric support such as `conf_area == "too_small"` or
    `conf_pix == "too_few_pix"`.
}

Filter 3 is recorded through \code{filter_3} and \code{reason_3}.

\strong{How the burned-like support reference is defined}

Spectral support in Filter 3 is always evaluated relative to a
keep-like reference distribution.

\strong{Local same-year fallback}

When `keep_pool = NULL`, the scoring engine derives that reference
internally from the same target year being scored.

More precisely, the local reference is built from the internal
same-year keep core defined by:
\itemize{
  \item candidate polygons entering the Stage-2 keep/review layer,
  \item restricted to polygons with `flag_internal == "keep"`.
}

This means the local same-year reference is based on the
seed-supported internal keep core of the target year. It is \emph{not}
derived from:
\itemize{
  \item final `class_final == "keep"` polygons,
  \item an external burned registry,
  \item or an externally validated burned map for that same year.
}

From this same-year keep core, the engine extracts finite
change-index pixel values and derives:
\itemize{
  \item `qref_keep`
  \item `q25_keep`
  \item `keep_medians`
  \item the empirical support distribution used for
    `percentile_in_keep`.
}

\strong{Explicit reference built from trusted external decision layers}

When `keep_pool` is supplied, Filter 3 uses that explicit external
reference directly and does not rebuild the local same-year fallback.

This explicit pool can be built from one or multiple trusted decision
layers, typically using:
\itemize{
  \item \code{\link[=collect_keep_reference_samples]{collect_keep_reference_samples()}}
  \item \code{\link[=build_keep_pool_from_samples]{build_keep_pool_from_samples()}}
}

In the current helper workflow, historical samples are selected using
a strict rule requiring:
\itemize{
  \item `class_final == "keep"`
  \item `filter_1 == "keep"`
  \item `filter_2 == "keep"`
  \item `filter_3 == "keep"`
  \item `conf_area == "ok"`
  \item `conf_pix == "ok"`
  \item minimum area compliance
  \item and no temporal drop when `preyear_action` is available.
}

This makes the explicit historical \code{keep_pool} stricter and more
externally controlled than the internal same-year fallback.

Helper-generated explicit pools retain \code{metadata$years_used}. If
those years include the current \code{target_year}, the public scorer
rejects the pool as an unsupported same-year explicit reference. In
that case, use \code{keep_pool = NULL} for the native same-year local
fallback.

\strong{Final deterministic integration}

The outputs of Filters 1, 2, and 3 are integrated into a final
deterministic confidence decision recorded in \code{class_final}.

In broad terms:
\itemize{
  \item fully consistent support leads to `keep`,
  \item mixed or unresolved evidence leads to `review`,
  \item strong contextual rejection leads to `drop`.
}

In the current canonical implementation, if exactly one active filter
drops a polygon but the remaining active filters keep it, the final
decision is softened to `review` rather than `drop`.
